1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,735
Lesson 27: stacking on two frequencies and on HF, Monte Carlo, updates

2
00:00:05,785 --> 00:00:17,369
YA-NEC course, lesson 27: stacking on two frequencies and on HF, Monte Carlo, updating and reporting a problem.

3
00:00:17,419 --> 00:00:30,737
Noise/GT/Stack, Stack spacings VHF. The model is the DK7ZB 7-element EME Yagi for 144 MHz.

4
00:00:30,787 --> 00:00:49,037
New in 1.5: the reference impedance (here 28 Ω, the antenna's own) and two frequencies, 144.1 and 145 MHz. I raise the maximum active SWR to 2: at 145 MHz the single antenna is already at 1.71.

5
00:00:49,087 --> 00:01:12,247
Worst case of the two: 0.55 times the DL6WU distance, 1.566 m. The stack gives 15.1 dBi with sidelobes at −13.4 dB; at the DL6WU distance it would be 16.3 dBi, but with sidelobes at −11.6 dB. The matrix shows the coupling between the ports.

6
00:01:12,297 --> 00:01:38,606
HF stacking over ground: HF stacking distances + HFTA. Example: the ARRL 520-40H Yagi for 20 m. You give the height of the lower antenna, the distances to try and the wanted take-off angles, 5 to 15 degrees.

7
00:01:38,656 --> 00:02:01,787
NEC computes every distance over the model's ground. Proposal: 18.47 m, that is 0.875 λ, with the upper antenna at 33.5 m: 17.12 dBi at 10 degrees, 2.59 dB more than the single antenna at 15 m. The chart compares in phase, top only, bottom only and out of phase.

8
00:02:01,837 --> 00:02:22,859
How to read the chart: all in phase (blue) peaks at 10 degrees and drops to zero near 25. There the other combinations help: out of phase (orange) gives about 16 dBi between 22 and 30 degrees, bottom only about 14.5 dBi between 12 and 22. With a switch the stack also covers the high angles.

9
00:02:22,909 --> 00:02:28,586
Now three antennas one above the other: click 3 and Calculate the HF stack again.

10
00:02:28,636 --> 00:02:48,588
Proposal: 13.2 m, that is 0.625 λ, with the antennas at 15, 28.2 and 41.4 m: 18.14 dBi at 9 degrees, 3.61 dB more than the single antenna. With three antennas the chart compares all in phase, top only and bottom only.

11
00:02:48,638 --> 00:03:05,748
Variables and optimization, Optimizer, Monte Carlo: it starts from several random points and checks the best candidates with copies that simulate building tolerances. The candidate with the most copies within the thresholds wins.

12
00:03:05,798 --> 00:03:20,138
Settings, Update: Force update checks GitHub right away, without waiting for the 3 minutes; Reinstall the current version downloads it again.

13
00:03:20,188 --> 00:03:31,538
Report a problem: describe what happened and YA-NEC prepares a zip with the open model and today's log. No IP addresses, e-mails or user names in the files.

14
00:03:31,588 --> 00:03:38,450
End of lesson 27. Chapter 26 of the manual lists everything new in 1.5.
